# A PARENTHESISED relative from-import -- `from . import (a, b,` / `c, d)`.
#
# THE FAILURE WAS SILENT AND REMOTE, which is why this is a fixture and not a
# note. PyParseImportRun's `from . import a, b` arm loops on
# `while CurTok.Kind = tkIdent`, so a leading '(' matched nothing: the loop body
# never ran, no diagnostic was produced, and the import bound no names at all.
# Every name then died at its FIRST USE as `undefined variable`, in another file
# and often hundreds of lines away from the import meant to bind it.
#
# Found on lekkerzeilen/app.py:18, which imports TWENTY submodules this way --
# the bare spelling does not fit a line, so a package of any size writes this
# one. It was the wall BEHIND capture.py's ctypes wall, and therefore invisible
# to a first-failure census: clearing ctypes is what made it reachable.
#
# Runs unmodified under CPython; the .expected is that run's output.
#
# The continuation line and the trailing comma are both load-bearing here.
# Newlines are skipped between elements because a bracketed list is implicitly
# continued, and the trailing comma leaves the parser on ')' instead of on an
# identifier -- the one position where an off-by-one in the new arm would show.
from nilpy_parenimp import joined

print(joined())
